            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Substantial air leakage is either confirmed or very likely in the following locations:



** The wall plugs &amp; switches in exterior walls, as well as the frame around the back exterior door is allowing air leakage &amp; heat loss. There is also air leakage along the header spaces where outside electrical outlets are located.



** There is very likely air leakage at all the joints between walls and ceilings and floors, which is typical for older houses. 



** In particular, there is very likely air leakage coming through at the front of your house, with the exposed overhang. This may be because of thermal bridging, a discontinuous air barrier at this location, and this area may be less well insulated than the rest of the house. 



** On review, there is a significant amount of air sealing work that could be done in your crawlspace, at the headers, between sheets of insulation, and to make sure the poly on the ground is sealed. This will reduce the cold air leaking in to your crawlspace. I also recommend that you seal the door to the crawlspace permanently, and use the entrance in the closet instead. 



** Your crawlspace venting should be sealed in the winter. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program if you complete a wall insulation upgrade.



I suspect for your home, achieving 20% or better as an improvement for the air leakage is quite achievable. 



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6.0 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modeled adding 3.0 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene to the exterior of your walls down to the top of the crawlspace concrete, although you could potentially add more insulation. 



Savings:

By increasing the insulation value of your walls by R15, you could potentially save $530 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 16.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <exposedFloors>

As you indicated that the exposed overhang at the front of your house is quite cold in the winter, I recommend that if you are upgrading the insulation in your exterior walls, that you increase the insulation in this overhang at the same time. </exposedFloors>
                <foundation />
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

I recommend you install an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92.You could potentially save $1,110 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current propane fired furnace was originally rated at 92.1% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Because your current propane furnace is already very efficient, I do not recommend that you upgrade your furnace at this time. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $140per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), 



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

BONUS REBATE

The AEA will provide a bonus rebate for any home that achieves a significant reduction in its annual energy us (measured in gigajoules).

- $1,000 for a 20% reduction in annual energy use

- $2,000 for a 30% reduction in annual energy use

- $3,000 for a 40% reduction in annual energy use

- $4,000 for a 50% reduction in annual energy use



Annual energy use will be measured by the AEA during pre-retrofit and post-retrofit home energy evaluations. 

As found on the first page of the report, your home currently rates at 144GJ. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

